# [Valid Sudoku](https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-sudoku/)

## Approach: Hash Set

- Iterate through every cell of the Sudoku board.
- For each cell, validate its column, row, and corresponding \(3 \times 3\) sub-box using an `unordered_set<char>`.
- Ignore empty cells represented by `'.'`.
- If a digit appears more than once in any row, column, or sub-box, return `false`.
- If every cell passes validation, return `true`.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool check(vector<vector<char>>& board, int row, int col) {
        int r = board.size(), c = board[0].size();
        unordered_set<char> st;

        // Check column
        for (int i = 0; i < r; i++) {
            char ch = board[i][col];
            if (ch == '.') continue;
            if (st.count(ch)) return false;
            st.insert(ch);
        }

        st.clear();

        // Check row
        for (int i = 0; i < c; i++) {
            char ch = board[row][i];
            if (ch == '.') continue;
            if (st.count(ch)) return false;
            st.insert(ch);
        }

        st.clear();

        // Check 3x3 sub-box
        int x = row - (row % 3);
        int y = col - (col % 3);

        for (int Row = x; Row < x + 3; Row++) {
            for (int Col = y; Col < y + 3; Col++) {
                char ch = board[Row][Col];
                if (ch == '.') continue;
                if (st.count(ch)) return false;
                st.insert(ch);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    bool isValid(vector<vector<char>>& board) {
        int row = board.size(), col = board[0].size();

        for (int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < col; j++) {
                if (!check(board, i, j)) return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    bool isValidSudoku(vector<vector<char>>& board) {
        return isValid(board);
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let \(n\) be the board's dimension.

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n^3)\), because each of the \(n^2\) cells triggers row, column, and sub-box checks costing \(O(n)\) in total.
- **Auxiliary Space:** \(O(n)\) for the hash set.

For the standard \(9 \times 9\) Sudoku board, both are effectively constant.

## Optimization

Your approach is correct for a standard \(9 \times 9\) board, but it repeatedly checks the same rows, columns, and boxes for every cell.

A more efficient approach uses three arrays of hash sets (or Boolean arrays) and checks each cell only once, achieving \(O(n^2)\) time. Also, the official LeetCode function signature is `isValidSudoku`, which your code correctly implements.
